  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$106.33B** of optical, measuring, precision and medical instruments in 2025, essentially flat on 2024 (+0.0%) and 27% above 2020. Medical, surgical and dental instruments (9018) were **35%** ($36.89B) and implants and orthopedic devices (9021) another 13%; laboratory analysis instruments (9027), measuring and inspection machines (9031) and electrical test instruments (9030) make up most of the rest. The EU is the largest market ($30.81B, 29%), led by the Netherlands ($12.12B); Canada and Mexico take another 20%.\n\nTariffs matter little here: Canada, Mexico, South Korea, Singapore and Australia charge nothing on qualifying goods, the EU went to zero on July 1, 2026, and Japan and the UK charge low rates. **China** is the exception: it adds a 10% extra duty on all U.S. goods and bought 11.7% less. What decides access is device approval (CE, NMPA, PMDA, Health Canada) and, for lasers, thermal imaging, navigation and some inspection tools, U.S. export controls.",
  "latestYear": 2025,
