  "keyTakeaways": [
    "U.S. instrument exports were flat at $106.33B in 2025; medical, surgical and dental instruments (9018, $36.89B) are 35% and the EU, led by the Netherlands, is 29% of the market.",
    "Canada, Mexico, South Korea, Singapore and Australia charge nothing on qualifying U.S. instruments, and the EU cut its remaining tariffs to zero on July 1, 2026.",
    "China is the only large buyer that raised tariffs (a 10% extra duty on all U.S. goods); U.S. exports there fell 11.7%. Its Board of Trade list names 43 medical-device lines for possible cuts, but none is published.",
    "Device approval — CE under the EU MDR, NMPA in China, PMDA in Japan, a Health Canada licence — is the real gate, and lasers, thermal imagers and navigation equipment need U.S. export licences for some destinations."
  ],
